// The final value at a location written through a pointer.
//
// MoRDor's own test, for github #5, "Test memory state in assertions".
//
// Thread 1 publishes the address of G through A; thread 2 reads it back and
// writes 7 through it. The write event is `W α 7` -- its location is the symbol
// read from A, not a location name -- so it lands on G only in the executions
// whose predicates pin α to G.
//
// The memory state the assertion is evaluated against used to be built by
// matching `Some (EVar var)` on the write's location and dropping everything
// else, so a write through a pointer had no entry at all and this outcome was
// unwitnessable. It now asks Solver.exeq whether the write's location must
// equal the one being asserted about, under the execution's own predicates.
//
// exeq and not expoteq: an execution that leaves the aliasing open is genuinely
// both cases and contributes nothing. atloc-noalias.lit is the control.
A := 0;
G := 0;
{
  rq := &G;
  A := rq
} ||| {
  rp := A;
  *rp := 7
}
%% allow (@G = 7) []
